 third  most climate vulnerable country in the  world and the first to feel the effects  of climate change are really the people  at the peripherals of society these are  the ones we live in the coastal areas  are fishermen the ones who are farming  and also those who live in urban poor or  marginalized communities the ones who  are in very vulnerable housing they feel  the effects immediately now these people  are here at this protest and they're  joined by the students who are saying  that they are also feeling the effects  of climate change because they fear for  their future they are disappointed that  the government is not sending a  representative to the COP talks this  December the president said that the  talks will call for emission our decline  in carbon emissions and that  disproportionately affects countries  like the Philippines who don't really  contribute to carbon emissions but the  youth are saying that's precisely where  our voices need to be heard that's  precisely where the Philippines a very  vulnerable country needs to stay that we  want our homes to be protected we want a  future okay and thank you very much for  that from Manila ana santos for us at  the protests in Manoa  well DWS Phoebe Kong is in the Taiwanese  capital Taipei she gave us this  assessment of what's happening there  today well now in downtown Taipei where  there is a group of students from  universities high schools and even  primary schools they are now holding a  press conference right here right behind  me and thousands of the students they  attend and participate in this press  conference during school time today and  the Reta our youth climate change  declarations which is to address the  climate change problems around the world  and they want to urge the politicians  especially the presidential elections  candidates to address this problem that  is to put this issue and put it to the  mutual agenda and platforms that is to  occur the greenhouse gases and and also  to control the temperature rise under  the 2015  Paris climate agreement so that is that  goal and this is the first activities to  check after one week long movement in  Taiwan so there will be a series of  protests and also street talks to  address the climate change problems on  the coming few days  well the initiator of the Friday's for  future strikes credit tune burgers to  spearhead a rally later today in New  York last month she traveled to the US  to be on hand for next week's climate  summit at the UN it was her appeals that  have mobilized so many young people  scientists environmental organizations  and politicians have been trying for  years to make the climate a matter of  urgency in vain Cikini old Vita two Moog  has done it turn Berg braved the open  sea on a two-week voyage aboard a yacht  from Europe to the US to bring her  message to the American president a  self-proclaimed climate skeptic in  person the media followed the trip  closely the climate and ecological  crisis is a global crisis and the  biggest crisis humanity has ever faced  and if we don't manage to work together  to cooperate and to to work together  despite our differences then we will  fail so we need to to stand together and  support each other and and to take  action it all started about a year ago  every Friday glutted windbag protested  in front of the Swedish parliament and  in so doing sparked the Friday's for  future movement just a few months later  the school girl mobilized millions of  people for the climate  suddenly young people all over the world  took to the streets like here in India  in Mexico or in Australia  or in Europe  [Applause]  after her arrival in New York at the end  of August to an bag made it clear at a  meeting with UN General Assembly  President Malia Espinosa  that she hoped the upcoming climate  Gathering would lead to concrete actions  to be some kind of breaking point where  people start to realize what is actually  going on and so we have high  expectations in YouTube and all Member  States to to deliver now tune Berg is  counting on the support of her fellow  campaigners all over the world and one  of those fellow campaigners is now with  us here in the studio  Clara Mayer of Friday's for future  supporter here in Berlin good morning  Clara good morning what do you want to  see come out of today's protests around  the world I hope that the world  politicians recognize us and that they  actually start acting that the UN  climate summit will come up with a real  solution against this crisis because  this crisis will affect every single one  of us okay now a lot of schools here in  Germany are giving students the day off  without penalty to take part in these  strikes why do you think this movement  has become so very strong here in  Germany well we've seen a lot of  stagnation the politics in Germany we've  had the same Chancellor for a long time  and she hasn't done much we are a  country with a lot of resources we are a  very wealthy country we have the  opportunities to become a leader against  the fight in the fight against climate  change but we are not and that is a  scandal and I think that's why people  are so frustrated especially in Germany  that the government isn't doing anything  although they have the capacity to so  okay you saw us talking to Taipei Taiwan  there and you saw the the young people  there there they're not as engaged as we  could see there is as we heard is the  young people here in Germany especially  if you look at the numbers out on the  streets every Friday here in Germany  what would you say to young people in  Taipei to Taiwan um I would say to them  that this is an issue that will affect  everyone on this planet and that I hope  that even though  they might not be striking today I hope  that they will in the future because  this issue will really affect every  single citizen on this planet okay as  part of that more and more students are  taking part in in the strike you know  there have been families fined for  allowing their children to go to to the  strikes and their sanctions threatened  for families in the future should their  children do do so the argument there is  that children should be learning and not  protesting out on a school day what's  your response to that I know people who  have been fined and I think it's  actually like a scandal because we  learned so much from the strikes this is  not about algebra this is about learning  about real Paul like world politics  every single one of us has learned so  much while striking this is about our  future our planet our lives and to  restrict people fighting for this cause  is a scandal in my opinion okay talking  about politics the German government is  looking to introduce a number of  measures to to reduce carbon emissions  here in Germany let's take a look at  that and talk to more than Germans love  to cycle they diligently recycle when it  comes to the environment Germans like to  consider themselves model citizens even  Angela Merkel was once hailed as the  climate Chancellor for pushing other  countries to acknowledge the scale of  climate change  Merkel's government also decided to  phase out nuclear energy by 2022 but  still Germany is no climate change role  model in fact it is one of the countries  with the highest co2 emissions worldwide  Germany wants to have these emissions by  2030 experts warn it won't meet this  target why it's early exit from nuclear  energy made Germany heavily reliant on  fossil fuels manufacturing transport and  other industries also contribute to  Germany's high emissions  how can Germany reduce its greenhouse  gases a minimum price for budget  airlines is under consideration other  ideas include improving public transport  to encourage people to use their cars  less the biggest thing Germany can do is  reduce its fossil fuel consumption  Germany only wants to phase out coal  energy by 2038  in the meantime it wants to tax carbon  dioxide a